_meanings:
That, (referring to a person
 or thing not present or near the speaker) ({#viprakfzwa#} or {#parokza#});
 {#idamastu sannikfzwaM samIpataravarti cEtado rUpam . adasastu viprakfzwaM#}
 {#taditi parokze vijAnIyAt .. amuzya vidyA rasanAgranartakI#} <ls>N. 1. 5</ls>; {#asO#}
 {#nAmA'hamasmIti svanAma parikIrtayet#} <ls>Ms. 2. 122</ls>. I am that person, so
 and so (giving the name); {#asAvahamiti brUyAt#} <ls n="Ms. 2.">130</ls>, <ls n="Ms. 2.">216</ls>; <ls>Y. 1. 26</ls>. {#adas#}
 is, however, often used with reference to {#pratyakza#} or {#sannikfzwa#}
 objects <ab>&c.</ab> in the sense of ‘this here’, ‘yonder’; {#asO SaraRyaH SaraRonmuKAnAm#}
 <ls>R. 6. 21</ls>. ({#asAviti purovartino nirdeSaH#} <ls>Malli.</ls>); {#amI raTyAH#} <ls>Ś. 1. 8</ls>;
 {#amI vahnayaH#} <ls n="Ś.">4. 18</ls>, <ls n="Ś.">7. 11</ls>. It is often used in the sense of
 {#tat#} as a correlative of {#yat#}; {#hiMsArataSca yo nityaM nehAsO suKameDate#}
 <ls>Ms. 4. 170</ls>. He, who <ab>&c.</ab> But when it immediately follows the relative pronoun
 ({#yo'sO, ye amI#} <ab>&c.</ab>) it conveys the sense of {#prasidDa#} ‘well-known’,
 ‘celebrated’, ‘renowned’; {#yo'sAvatIndriyagrAhyaH sUkzmo'vyaktaH sanAtanaH#}
 <ls>Ms. 1. 7</ls>; {#yo'sO kumArasevako nAma#} <ls>Mu. 3</ls>; {#yo'sO coraH#} <ls>Dk. 68</ls>;
 sometimes {#adas#} used by itself conveys this sense; {#viDurapi viDiyogAd#}
 {#grasyate rAhuRA'sO#} that (so well-known to us all) moon too. See the
 word {#tad#} also and the quotations from <ls>K. P.</ls>
